## What was wrong

The script half was complete and verified; CLAUDE.md was never touched. The census number — which the task itself calls "the deliverable ... and it decides the milestone" — existed only in a script's stdout and would have been gone next session.

## What I did

Added two paragraphs to CLAUDE.md, placed directly after the check_assertion_weakening.py bullet, since this is explicitly its sibling (static-diff half → verdict half).

I re-derived every number myself rather than transcribing the evaluator's feedback, then cross-checked the doc against a fresh run programmatically — a transcription slip can't be caught by reading, because a wrong number agrees with itself:

```
key         live   claim-found-in-CLAUDE.md
window      52     OK      task    12    OK
notest      5      OK      addr     7    OK
reg         5      OK      beh      2    OK
addr_rate   58     OK      beh_rate 17   OK
RESULT: ALL NUMBERS AGREE WITH LIVE RUN
```

The bullet carries all five required elements: the five states (none folded), the compile-vs-assert discriminator plus **why `UNEARNED` is arithmetically unreachable without it** (the evt-0011 parallel), the three landmines, the measured census with its window, and the limit.

## Two things I was careful not to overstate

The census is reported as the unflattering finding it is. The headline is the 17% behavioural rate, not the 58% addressable one, because 5 of the 7 addressable commits are tests/module_size.rs register bookkeeping that fails by construction whenever a module grows — counting those would manufacture UNEARNEDs out of the gate working as designed. With 2 behavioural commits in 52, the honest reading is that the milestone as written is close to unmeasurable at this scope, and I wrote that rather than talking it up.

The live reading was not taken, and the bullet says so. It was the stated stretch, not the gate; recording it as not run rather than letting silence read as clean is the same refusal the rest of the instrument makes.

